#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# parallel_table

#% text
# `parallel_table(e, k, r)` gives the list of the values of `e` for each `k` in `r`, as `table(e, k, r)` does, with the values computed on the session's worker threads.

#% text
# `parallel_table(e, k1, r1, k2, r2, ...)` gives nested lists, the outermost iterator split over the threads.

#% text
# `parallel_table(e, n)` gives `n` values of `e`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The result is exactly the result of `table` with the same arguments: the same values in the same order, failures kept as elements.

#% item
# The values are split at once into runs of about n/(4 threads) elements, one run per task, whatever each value costs; `table` splits only when its values are found to be expensive.

#% item
# Printed output and assignments inside the values happen in the order of the serial evaluation: a value that prints or assigns is evaluated again in order after the parallel part, so such values gain nothing.

#% item
# The worker threads are the session's (`--threads` for `mc-repl`, up to 4 by default). With one thread the values are computed one after another.

#% item
# An interrupt, or the fuel, time or memory limit, stops every worker.

#% item
# `table(e, k, r, parallel: true)` is the same as `parallel_table(e, k, r)`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# Squares, computed in parallel:

#% cell
parallel_table(k^2, k, 8)
#% output
#= [1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49, 64]

#% text
# Over the elements of a list:

#% cell
parallel_table(gcd(k, 12), k, [3, 8, 9, 10])
#% output
#= [3, 4, 3, 2]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Two iterators give a matrix:

#% cell
parallel_table(i + j, i, 2, j, 3)
#% output
#= [[2, 3, 4], [3, 4, 5]]

#% text
# A count alone repeats the expression:

#% cell
parallel_table(x, 3)
#% output
#= [x, x, x]

#% subsection
# Options

#% text
# The option form of `table`:

#% cell
table(k^3, k, 5, parallel: true)
#% output
#= [1, 8, 27, 64, 125]

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# A parameter scan: each value solves an equation numerically.

#% cell
parallel_table(x /. find_root(cos(x) == a*x, [x, 1]), a, [0.5, 1.0, 2.0])
#% output
#= [1.0298665293222589, 0.7390851332151607, 0.45018361129487355]

#% text
# Integrals for a range of frequencies:

#% cell
parallel_table(n_integrate(sin(w*t)^2*exp(-t), [t, 0, 10]), w, [1, 2, 3])
#% output
#= [0.39997086319476105, 0.47056066499741983, 0.48646432423514996]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The values are those of `table`:

#% cell
parallel_table(prime(k), k, 20) == table(prime(k), k, 20)
#% output
#= true

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# Values that print run in order after the parallel part, so they are not faster than with `table`:

#% cell
parallel_table({ print(k); k }, k, 3)
#% output
#| 1
#| 2
#| 3
#= [1, 2, 3]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `table`, `parallel_map`, `parallel_sum`, `map`, `profile`
